{{also|Appendix:Variations of "z"}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
[[eye dialect|Eye dialect]] spelling variant of {{m|en|-s}}.

====Suffix====
{{head|en|suffix}}

# {{lb|en|urban|_|slang|[[lolspeak]]}} {{ngd|Used as a substitute for [[-s]] in marking the plural of nouns. Usually used in words in which the [[-s]] suffix is actually pronounced {{IPAchar|/z/}}.}}
#: {{ux|en|Boy'''z''' are always trouble.}}
#: {{ux|en|Oh hai. In teh beginnin Ceiling Cat maded teh skie'''z''' An da Urfs|q=Gen 1:1, LOLcat Bible}}
# {{lb|en|urban|_|slang}} {{ngd|Used as a substitute for [[-s]] in marking verb inflections.}}
#: {{ux|en|He love'''z''' me.}}

===Etymology 2===
{{glossary|Rhotacism}} of {{IPAchar|/-ɹ/}}

Documented since at least 1987.<ref>{{cite-book|author=John Silverlight|title=More Words|publisher=Macmillan|year=1987|page=53|isbn=9780333436844|passage=Examples are 'Kaz', 'Shaz', 'Baz' and Waz' for Karen, Sharon, Barry, and Warwick, and the latest, 'soz' for sorry.}}</ref>

====Suffix====
{{head|en|suffix}}

# {{lb|en|UK|Australia|NZ}} {{n-g|Forms nicknames, especially of personal names.}}
#: {{m|en|Barry}} &rarr; {{m|en|Baz}}, {{m|en|Sharon}} &rarr; {{m|en|Shaz}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Applied to words whose first syllable ends in {{IPAchar|/-ɹ/}}, with the suffix replacing the {{IPAchar|/ɹ/}} and the rest of the word.

=====Derived terms=====
*[[-zza]]
*[[soz]]

